 It throws when the patch is invalid (not an array, unknown `op`, missing `value` or `from`, bad or missing `path`/`from` pointer, sparse array) or when
 an operation fails (missing target, array index out of range, failed `test`, removing the whole document,
 moving a value into its own child).
+
+## JSON Merge Patch
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` (RFC 7386) applies a JSON Merge Patch and returns the merged document as a new
+value; the inputs are not mutated. Object members merge recursively, a `null` member removes the target's member,
+and anything else (arrays and scalars included) replaces it:
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+applyMergePatch({ a: { b: 1, c: 2 }, d: [1] }, { a: { b: null, e: 3 }, d: [2] }); // { a: { c: 2, e: 3 }, d: [2] }
+applyMergePatch({ a: 1 }, [1]); // [1] (a non-object patch replaces the whole document)
+```
+
+It never throws: any JSON value is a valid patch.
